Source package: mesa

llvmpipe-fence-signalled-lock.patch
    llvmpipe, Mesa's software renderer, signals a fence from each of its rasterizer threads with lp_fence_signal(),
    which increments the fence's count with the fence's mutex held, and then broadcasts its condition variable and
    unlocks the mutex. lp_fence_signalled() read the count without the mutex, so it could report the fence signalled
    while the last rasterizer thread was still between the increment and the unlock. Its callers drop their reference
    to a signalled fence, for example lp_setup_get_empty_scene() when it reuses a scene, and the last reference frees
    the fence, 120 bytes with the mutex at offset 16. The late pthread_mutex_unlock() then writes zero over the 4 bytes
    at offset 24 of an object allocated there in the meantime, and decrements the 4 bytes at offset 28. The patch reads
    the count with the mutex held, which orders the check after the unlock of the last signalling thread.

    GNOME Shell runs with llvmpipe on a machine without a GPU, as in the session tests (tests/session/), where it
    crashed about once in 70 logins, about a second after starting. St renders a box shadow through llvmpipe in
    _st_create_shadow_pipeline() and then allocates a CoglPipelineLayer, which reused the freed fence's memory, and the
    unlock overwrote the layer's parent pointer. With an LD_PRELOAD allocator which put every allocation of 105 to 120
    bytes on a page of its own and made the page inaccessible on free(), a rasterizer thread faulted in
    rasterize_scene(), unlocking the freed fence, in 2 of 135 logins of the global-menu session tests, 3 sessions in
    parallel. With the patch, none of 270 logins faulted or crashed.

    The patch has no test of its own, as the race needs a rasterizer thread to stop between two statements, which a
    test cannot arrange without changes to llvmpipe. The fix is proposed upstream in
    https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/mesa/mesa/-/merge_requests/46712. Drop the patch once Ubuntu ships a Mesa which
    includes it.
